WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.636 --> 00:00:02.331
- Good morning Believe
Nation, today's message

00:00:02.331 --> 00:00:05.489
is live and breathe your craft.

00:00:05.489 --> 00:00:07.480
Over to you Martin Garrix.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:08.966
(rooster crowing)

00:00:08.966 --> 00:00:12.339
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:12.339 --> 00:00:14.522
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:14.522 --> 00:00:18.022
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:19.259 --> 00:00:21.882
- When I was like eight year old,

00:00:21.882 --> 00:00:25.040
I was playing soccer and
like the guys on my team

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.943
didn't take it seriously, it
was like oh you want to be a DJ?

00:00:27.943 --> 00:00:29.026
I don't know,

00:00:30.753 --> 00:00:34.259
I love producing and I love DJing.

00:00:34.259 --> 00:00:36.743
So I always kept on doing it, practicing,

00:00:36.743 --> 00:00:38.276
trying to get better.

00:00:38.276 --> 00:00:42.014
- How many hours a day
are you busy with music?

00:00:42.014 --> 00:00:44.661
- All the hours that I'm not asleep.

00:00:44.661 --> 00:00:47.053
Literally I wake up
also listening to music.

00:00:47.053 --> 00:00:49.220
Music is everything to me.

00:00:50.722 --> 00:00:52.254
- [Interviewer] You live and breathe it.

00:00:52.254 --> 00:00:54.390
- Here's a guy, Martin
Garrix, 20 years old,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.057
listed to be the number
one DJ in the world.

00:01:00.544 --> 00:01:03.260
How can you achieve massive success

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.397
in a short amount of time?

00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:09.414
You have to live and breathe your craft.

00:01:09.414 --> 00:01:11.805
Too many people are not prolific enough.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:14.336
The people who are the best
in the world at what they do,

00:01:14.336 --> 00:01:15.474
they're prolific.

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:17.866
They practice, they repeat over and over

00:01:17.866 --> 00:01:19.909
and over and over again in all fields.

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:21.535
How many times have you
seen the best athlete

00:01:21.535 --> 00:01:24.483
in whatever sport being the
first person to be at the gym

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:26.225
and the last person to leave.

00:01:26.225 --> 00:01:27.943
It's almost a cliche.

00:01:27.943 --> 00:01:29.545
But it happens every time.

00:01:29.545 --> 00:01:30.915
Michael Jordan had the most success,

00:01:30.915 --> 00:01:33.145
Michael Jordan took the
most free throw practices,

00:01:33.145 --> 00:01:34.840
took the most jump shot practices.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.720
He practiced, he practiced, he practiced

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.367
to hone the natural talent that he had

00:01:39.367 --> 00:01:42.363
and make it something extraordinary.

00:01:42.363 --> 00:01:44.058
You can't coast alone on talent.

00:01:44.058 --> 00:01:45.590
If you do you'll be mediocre at best.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.634
You have to be able to put in the work.

00:01:47.634 --> 00:01:49.097
So here's this guy 20 years old,

00:01:49.097 --> 00:01:51.047
best DJ in the world because he lives

00:01:51.047 --> 00:01:53.114
and he breathes his craft.

00:01:53.114 --> 00:01:54.716
You can get there in a short
amount of time if you hustle.

00:01:54.716 --> 00:01:58.083
I've told the story about
myself at the agency

00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:00.962
that I was at where I was
the worst storyteller,

00:02:00.962 --> 00:02:03.516
I was the worst person on camera.

00:02:03.516 --> 00:02:05.235
In a couple months became the best

00:02:05.235 --> 00:02:06.790
just because I was prolific,

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:08.462
not because I had skill or talent,

00:02:08.462 --> 00:02:10.761
but because I worked harder.

00:02:10.761 --> 00:02:12.897
I'd be making multiple videos a day

00:02:12.897 --> 00:02:15.242
were the people there had a hard time

00:02:15.242 --> 00:02:16.821
finding time to make one video a week.

00:02:16.821 --> 00:02:19.213
If you're only doing
something one day a week,

00:02:19.213 --> 00:02:20.908
it's going to be really, really hard

00:02:20.908 --> 00:02:22.232
for you to get better at it.

00:02:22.232 --> 00:02:24.159
And the people who really
live and breathe their craft

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:27.154
are going to crush you, they're
going to blow right past you.

00:02:27.154 --> 00:02:29.012
And that's sucks if you're that person,

00:02:29.012 --> 00:02:31.148
but it's also great if you are the person

00:02:31.148 --> 00:02:33.284
who live and breathe
'cause you can pass people

00:02:33.284 --> 00:02:36.535
very quickly who have a lot
more experience than you

00:02:36.535 --> 00:02:39.693
because you are putting
in the work to get better.

00:02:39.693 --> 00:02:41.574
When I was doing my bio video story,

00:02:41.574 --> 00:02:43.455
did it 30, 40 times.

00:02:43.455 --> 00:02:44.732
I would do it three times a day,

00:02:44.732 --> 00:02:46.264
I would send it to my
agent in the morning,

00:02:46.264 --> 00:02:47.541
he would give me feedback,

00:02:47.541 --> 00:02:48.818
I'd do it again at lunch,
he'd give me feedback,

00:02:48.818 --> 00:02:50.444
I'd do it again in the evening,
he would give me feedback

00:02:50.444 --> 00:02:52.394
and I would make it better and
better and better and better.

00:02:52.394 --> 00:02:53.764
And those were long videos.

00:02:53.764 --> 00:02:55.227
Those were like 20 minute videos.

00:02:55.227 --> 00:02:56.829
It wasn't just two minutes.

00:02:56.829 --> 00:02:58.640
I redid the whole thing each time

00:02:58.640 --> 00:02:59.987
and I sent it to him and he watched it

00:02:59.987 --> 00:03:02.123
and he gave me feedback and
I did it again and again.

00:03:02.123 --> 00:03:04.863
I'm going through this
process with Lilly now

00:03:04.863 --> 00:03:07.394
where we're starting this
project of unlocking Lilly

00:03:07.394 --> 00:03:09.461
to help turn her into a public speaker.

00:03:09.461 --> 00:03:10.294
We're doing the same thing.

00:03:10.294 --> 00:03:13.701
Her first video she shot almost 100 times.

00:03:13.701 --> 00:03:16.000
Then she sent me a version
and I said do it again.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.602
Sent me another version, do it again.

00:03:17.602 --> 00:03:19.158
Other versions she didn't send me

00:03:19.158 --> 00:03:20.435
because she knew it wasn't very good

00:03:20.435 --> 00:03:22.223
and she wanted to redo it
and tweek and tweek and tweek

00:03:22.223 --> 00:03:24.103
and tweek it and still
this is nothing compared

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.473
to where she's going to be able to be.

00:03:25.473 --> 00:03:26.843
But she hasn't quit her job
yet, she's working full time

00:03:26.843 --> 00:03:29.398
and she's still finding time.

00:03:29.398 --> 00:03:31.000
She said before I go to work tomorrow,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.331
I'm going to go outside
and I'm going to film it.

00:03:32.331 --> 00:03:33.916
When I get home tonight,
before it gets dark,

00:03:33.916 --> 00:03:35.193
I'm going to film another version.

00:03:35.193 --> 00:03:36.726
She's grinding, she's hustling,

00:03:36.726 --> 00:03:38.537
so she's going to get better faster.

00:03:38.537 --> 00:03:41.254
You have to live and breathe your craft.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.344
If you want to be the best
in the world at what you do,

00:03:43.344 --> 00:03:45.340
you have to practice.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.128
It's not going to come
from just doing something

00:03:47.128 --> 00:03:48.378
one day a week.

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:51.494
So the question today is

00:03:51.494 --> 00:03:54.234
what is your craft,
what are you beating on

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.695
every single day to try to get better

00:03:56.695 --> 00:03:58.994
so you can be one of the best
in the world at what you do,

00:03:58.994 --> 00:04:00.364
what is it?

00:04:00.364 --> 00:04:02.593
Leave it in the comments
below, I'm curious to find out.

00:04:02.593 --> 00:04:04.729
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Rick Verwimp.

00:04:04.729 --> 00:04:06.912
Rick thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:04:06.912 --> 00:04:09.327
Your One Word, I really
appreciate the support.

00:04:09.327 --> 00:04:10.859
I hope you're enjoying the book

00:04:10.859 --> 00:04:12.647
and you're applying some
of the lessons to your life

00:04:12.647 --> 00:04:14.481
and to your business.

00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:15.480
Thank you so much.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.664
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:04:16.664 --> 00:04:18.197
I hope you have an amazing day.

00:04:18.197 --> 00:04:19.660
I believe in you and hope
you continue to believe

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:22.051
in yourself and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:04:22.051 --> 00:04:25.116
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:04:25.116 --> 00:04:27.670
♫ I wake up every morning

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:31.432
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:04:31.432 --> 00:04:35.194
- So what advice would you give purely

00:04:35.194 --> 00:04:37.492
in an acting sense to yourself

00:04:37.492 --> 00:04:38.909
30, 40 years ago?

00:04:41.579 --> 00:04:45.852
- Same advice I gave
myself 30, 40 years ago.

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:47.036
Get a job.

00:04:47.036 --> 00:04:49.286
(laughter)

00:04:50.983 --> 00:04:51.816
Act.

00:04:53.189 --> 00:04:54.022
There are

00:04:56.858 --> 00:04:59.342
unfortunately those of us
who say well I want to be

00:04:59.342 --> 00:05:00.175
a star.

00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:07.072
That's

00:05:08.375 --> 00:05:11.161
way out there in the ether somewhere.

00:05:11.161 --> 00:05:15.457
I want to be a working
actor, you can pull that off.

00:05:15.457 --> 00:05:16.540
So act, work.

00:05:18.452 --> 00:05:19.285
Work,

00:05:20.496 --> 00:05:24.072
someone asked me once and
you're about to do it,

00:05:24.072 --> 00:05:26.556
(laughter)

00:05:26.556 --> 00:05:29.877
What would you do if you weren't an actor?

00:05:29.877 --> 00:05:32.408
If you didn't make it as an actor,

00:05:32.408 --> 00:05:33.908
what would you do?

00:05:35.264 --> 00:05:37.214
I have no idea.

00:05:37.214 --> 00:05:39.861
I would act somewhere.

00:05:39.861 --> 00:05:41.649
Maybe I'd be driving a cab,

00:05:41.649 --> 00:05:44.227
maybe I'm working somebody's yard,

00:05:44.227 --> 00:05:46.433
whatever I'm doing, I'm
going to belong to somebody's

00:05:46.433 --> 00:05:49.183
little theater group, I will act.

00:05:51.309 --> 00:05:54.025
'Cause I'll die if I don't.

00:05:54.025 --> 00:05:58.391
- Watch as many movies of
as many eras as you can.

00:05:58.391 --> 00:06:01.200
When I was coming up through the ranks

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.358
there was a thing called
a cinema club in the 70s

00:06:04.358 --> 00:06:06.750
where you thread a 60 millimeter film

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.419
and you projected it and
I became familiar with

00:06:10.419 --> 00:06:13.669
people that are important to me like _,

00:06:14.947 --> 00:06:18.030
like Lauren Hardy, the Three Stooges.

00:06:19.196 --> 00:06:21.588
You became familiar with _,

00:06:21.588 --> 00:06:24.838
you became familiar with
everything from comedy

00:06:24.838 --> 00:06:25.671
to drama.

00:06:26.789 --> 00:06:30.458
You can see Preston Sturgis,
don't just limit yourself to

00:06:30.458 --> 00:06:34.625
what is out there or the latest
top ten in the box office.

00:06:35.752 --> 00:06:37.540
That's the main tool.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.623
And by live film history.

00:06:41.812 --> 00:06:43.345
Because you're part of that now.

00:06:43.345 --> 00:06:45.574
Even if it's not film anymore,

00:06:45.574 --> 00:06:48.639
you're part of that narrative tradition.

00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:51.123
The second thing I feel very important,

00:06:51.123 --> 00:06:55.558
do not get wrapped in the
industry side of this.

00:06:55.558 --> 00:06:57.695
That's what I find really heartbreaking

00:06:57.695 --> 00:07:00.411
that people are now a lot
of times you're discussing

00:07:00.411 --> 00:07:02.803
film with people that love film

00:07:02.803 --> 00:07:05.380
and it's like you're listening to an agent

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:08.515
from an agency or a head of a studio.

00:07:08.515 --> 00:07:11.092
They know the grosses,
they know who's doing this.

00:07:11.092 --> 00:07:13.759
That's absolutely not important.

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:17.223
The important thing is that
tradition you're going to

00:07:17.223 --> 00:07:21.077
belong to and do not get
caught on the other side.

00:07:21.077 --> 00:07:23.794
Stay true to the other side that needs you

00:07:23.794 --> 00:07:26.185
and needs to _ urgently.

00:07:26.185 --> 00:07:28.670
- By the time I got to college

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:31.131
I made this album called
the Language of My World.

00:07:31.131 --> 00:07:33.214
(cheers)

00:07:34.893 --> 00:07:37.192
And with this album

00:07:37.192 --> 00:07:39.073
I did the same thing, I
locked myself in my room

00:07:39.073 --> 00:07:42.068
for like almost college, that was it.

00:07:42.068 --> 00:07:44.655
I just worked my ass off in my room

00:07:44.655 --> 00:07:47.557
and kept writing, kept
recording and I wasn't great

00:07:47.557 --> 00:07:50.274
going into college but
by the time I got out,

00:07:50.274 --> 00:07:54.274
I had kind of learned a
little bit of the craft.

00:07:55.916 --> 00:07:58.052
And by the time that I got out of college

00:07:58.052 --> 00:08:02.219
I read this book called
Outliers by Malcolm Gladwell.

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:06.830
Nobody here's read it.

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:09.988
One person, anyway this book examines,

00:08:09.988 --> 00:08:12.905
this book examines this ferry right

00:08:14.678 --> 00:08:16.396
and Malcolm Gladwell makes this point,

00:08:16.396 --> 00:08:18.602
he says it's not about what _,

00:08:18.602 --> 00:08:20.158
it's not about the talent
that you're born with,

00:08:20.158 --> 00:08:23.896
it's about the hours that
you put into that talent.

00:08:23.896 --> 00:08:26.033
And he says that around,
if you want to be a master

00:08:26.033 --> 00:08:27.751
of whatever it is that you love to do,

00:08:27.751 --> 00:08:30.909
if you wanted to develop that
craft to its upmost potential,

00:08:30.909 --> 00:08:32.348
what you've got to do is work.

00:08:32.348 --> 00:08:34.647
You have to work a lot,
you have to put in hours.

00:08:34.647 --> 00:08:37.735
And he says that if you
put in around 10,000 hours,

00:08:37.735 --> 00:08:41.218
just about one thing
that eventually you will

00:08:41.218 --> 00:08:42.718
master that craft.

00:08:45.398 --> 00:08:47.372
I don't know how close
I am to 10,000 hours

00:08:47.372 --> 00:08:50.088
but every single time we step on stage,

00:08:50.088 --> 00:08:52.005
we get one step closer.

